## Smoke test 1 — cobblestone-qemu

**What it proves:** run Codex on real x86 and tell me what came out. Four
subjects through the seed under QEMU, then four checkers on fib.

### Raw U57 first, to see the failure

Both our defects are live at `49fa9f27`, measured rather than inferred:

    fib       BUILT  0m07s          the transport works, the seed boots
    zigemit   RED    zigemit.zig:3891:9: error: local variable shadows
                                     declaration of 'cx_new'

`codexir` was NOT run at raw U57. It emits the same prelude and would fail on
the identical line, so the run would cost five minutes to confirm arithmetic.
The build stops at the first red by design and there is nothing to learn from
proving a shared failure twice.

### u58-candidate, and there is no third defect

    ring plug   0m26s      7442 lines, 390080 bytes
    fib         0m07s
    zigemit     0m41s
    codexir     4m35s      (compile 2m46s, transpile 1m44s)
    x86emit     6m26s      (compile 3m06s, transpile 3m14s)

    verify zig    PASS   native codexir | zigemit: fib prints 55 and 610
    verify qemu   PASS   bare metal: IR identical to the native tool
    verify x86    PASS   emitted machine code runs
    verify boot   PASS   emitted x86-64 BOOTS and prints 6765
    GREEN 4/4

**The two fixes are sufficient at U57 exactly as they were at U56.**

### The ring plug is byte-identical across the two releases

`ringplug-source.codex` is 390564 bytes at BOTH `u57-candidate` (over U56) and
`u58-candidate` (over U57), with the same source list and no diff. Every
chapter it bundles -- `Core/Name`, `Core/SourceText`, `Types/CodexType`,
`Ast/AstNodes`, `IR/IRChapter`, `plugs/common/PlugTypes`,
`plugs/common/IRTextParser`, and the Zig Emitter -- is untouched by U57.

So the plug is literally the same program at both pins, and any difference
between the two runs is the SEED's. Corroborated by the compile diagnostics
being identical too: 26 CDX4010, 4 CDX3005, 1 CDX4030 at each.

### Timings say nothing moved structurally

    stage              U56+ours   U57+ours
    ring plug             0m25s      0m26s
    zigemit compile       0m25s      0m26s
    codexir compile       2m39s      2m46s
    codexir transpile     1m43s      1m44s
    x86emit compile       2m54s      3m06s
    x86emit transpile     3m14s      3m14s

Every stage within a few percent, consistent with a seed that grew from
3,217,563 to 3,322,781 bytes. **U57 is marginally SLOWER, not faster** -- the
first reading of this table compared U57's compile step against U56's subject
TOTAL and read as a 40% improvement that does not exist.

### The x86 change is real and invisible to fib, which is correct

The emitted x86 for fib is byte-identical at both pins: 287,765 bytes of dump,
26 symbols, 89,677 reassembled. A difference was registered as EXPECTED before
the run because `Emit/X86_64*.codex` moved; there is none, and the reason is
specific rather than a shrug.

The change is COMPILER-20's over-application miscompile:

    -    is FunTy (pt) (row) (rt) -> emit-partial-application st name [] (1 + fun-type-arity rt)
    +     let ua = lookup-x86-arity (st.user-arities) name
    +     in emit-partial-application st name [] (if ua > 0 then ua else 1 + fun-type-arity rt)

plus `emit-over-push-extras`, `emit-trampoline-over-dynamic` and
`emit-over-apply-loop` in `X86_64Compound.codex`, and a new test
`codex/test/ops/saturated-call-returning-function.codex`. **fib applies nothing
over its arity**, so identical bytes is the right answer.

COMPILER-20 was our PR 90, and over-application is a lane we opened (PRs 79, 80,
83, 86). The four fib checkers assert fib's ANSWER, not emitted bytes, so this
arm cannot regress on a legitimate back-end change -- and equally cannot see one.

## Two gaps in our own tooling, found by using it

**The .pyc guard covered two ways in and not the third.** Running
`python3 zig_plug_pages.py` by hand -- which is how `bundle_ringplug.ps1` calls
it -- wrote `roots.cpython-312.pyc` into the checkout. `-B` in a shebang applies
to `./name.py` alone and `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE` in `build.sh` covers only
what build.sh spawns, so every `.ps1` call was already outside the guarantee.
The empty `.gitignore` surfaced it in one `git status`, which is what it is for.

The four verifiers already set `sys.dont_write_bytecode` before importing, with
the reason written out; the four scripts that import a local module did not.
They do now, so the guarantee no longer depends on how python was started.

**`run_all.py` could not check a sandbox it did not build.** The checkers read
`$SANDBOX` and always could, but the orchestrator coupled build and check, so a
`build.sh` run had no path to a verdict -- and a sandbox with no RESULT is
documented here as broken. `SANDBOX` naming a directory that already has a
PROVENANCE now means check that one, the same contract `build.sh` already uses.
The verdict above took 10 seconds instead of a 13-minute rebuild.

RESULT now also names the CHECKER sha, because PROVENANCE stamps the qemu sha
at BUILD time and a check-only run can be a different one:

    codex     8570fba1...    branch u58-candidate
    verdict   GREEN          checkers 99aea4593ea9
    built     not by this run
